Chapter: E1000CtrlRo
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Kernel chapter E1000e
  cites Kernel chapter Pci
  cites Dev chapter NicAsde

 CTRL is read-only to the driver on Damian's Intel I219-V, measured on
 metal 2026-08-13: the probe cleared CTRL.SLU and read CTRL straight back
 four times across two flights and got 0x180240, the firmware value, every
 time. MDIC writes work on that same part, so this is CTRL and not the CSR
 write path. -e1000-ctrl-ro is the arm that reproduces it.

 The row that pays is settled-lies-about-reset. With CTRL discarded,
 e1000-reset never sets RST, so e1000-await-reset sees it clear on its
 first read and answers 1 -- a reset that never happened, reporting as one
 that completed. That is what read as "the reset works on a warm part" on
 2026-08-13, and no aggregate can tell the two apart.

Section: Fixtures

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

  clear-slu : Integer = #FFFFFFFF - e1000-ctrl-slu

Section: Judges

 A write that is refused leaves the bit it tried to clear still set.

  slu-write-refused : PciDevice -> Boolean
  slu-write-refused (dev) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in let c0 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
   in let w = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-ctrl (bit-and c0 clear-slu)
   in bit-and (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl) e1000-ctrl-slu /= 0

 Firmware left SLU set, and the refusal is what preserves it.

  ctrl-is-firmware-value : PciDevice -> Boolean
  ctrl-is-firmware-value (dev) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl == #180240

  asde-does-not-stick : PciDevice -> Boolean
  asde-does-not-stick (dev) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in let arm = na-bring-up-after mmio (dev.pci-device-id) True
   in bit-and (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl) e1000-ctrl-asde == 0

 The link comes up anyway, because na-phy-kick reaches the PHY over MDIO
 and the CTRL write was never load-bearing.

  link-up-without-ctrl : PciDevice -> Boolean
  link-up-without-ctrl (dev) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in (na-bring-up-after mmio (dev.pci-device-id) False).na-gave == na-gave-ok

  mdic-still-writable : PciDevice -> Boolean
  mdic-still-writable (dev) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in na-phy-kick mmio (dev.pci-device-id) == 1

 settled=1 from a reset that never ran. This row reads the SAME with the
 arm and without it, deliberately, and that identity IS the finding: no
 aggregate distinguishes a reset that completed from one that was
 discarded, so a green here is not evidence a reset happened. Do not
 "fix" it into a discriminating row; the point is that it cannot be one.

  reset-answers-one-either-way : PciDevice -> Boolean
  reset-answers-one-either-way (dev) =
   let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in e1000-reset mmio == 1

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let scan = pci-scan-bus 0
    in act
      when e1000-find scan
        is Just (dev) -> act
          print-line-uni ("slu write refused: " & yn (slu-write-refused dev))
          print-line-uni ("ctrl is firmware : " & yn (ctrl-is-firmware-value dev))
          print-line-uni ("asde not sticking: " & yn (asde-does-not-stick dev))
          print-line-uni ("mdic still writes: " & yn (mdic-still-writable dev))
          print-line-uni ("link up anyway   : " & yn (link-up-without-ctrl dev))
          print-line-uni ("reset says 1 too: " & yn (reset-answers-one-either-way dev))
        end
        is None -> print-line-uni ("candidate found : no")
    end
  end

